{
 "id": "mc-dc-machines",
 "title": "DC Machines",
 "description": "Construction, back EMF and torque, the equivalent circuit, and separately excited and shunt motors.",
 "reference": "Hambley §16.3–16.4",
 "study": [
  {
   "title": "Construction",
   "body": "The **stator** carries the field poles: a field winding fed with DC (or permanent magnets). The **rotor** (armature) has conductors in slots, connected to a **commutator**: copper segments that slide under stationary carbon **brushes**. The commutator reverses the current in each armature coil as it passes from one pole to the next, so the torque always acts in the same direction. Brushes and commutator wear, so DC machines need more maintenance than AC machines."
  },
  {
   "title": "Induced voltage and torque",
   "body": "The armature's rotation in the field induces the **back EMF** $E_A$, proportional to flux and speed. The torque is proportional to flux and armature current. The same machine constant $K\\phi$ appears in both (V·s/rad = N·m/A), because the developed power is $E_AI_A = T_{dev}\\omega_m$.",
   "formula": "E_A = K\\phi\\,\\omega_m,\\qquad T_{dev} = K\\phi\\,I_A,\\qquad P_{dev} = E_AI_A = T_{dev}\\,\\omega_m"
  },
  {
   "title": "Magnetization curve",
   "body": "$E_A$ measured at no load against field current $I_F$ at a fixed speed. It rises linearly and then flattens as the iron **saturates**. Because $E_A \\propto \\omega_m$ for a given $I_F$, a curve measured at speed $n_1$ gives the EMF at any other speed:",
   "formula": "\\frac{E_{A2}}{E_{A1}} = \\frac{n_2}{n_1}\\quad(\\text{same } I_F)",
   "plot": {
    "x": {
     "min": 0,
     "max": 6,
     "label": "I_F (A)",
     "ticks": [
      0,
      1,
      2,
      3,
      4,
      5,
      6
     ]
    },
    "y": {
     "min": 0,
     "max": 330.0,
     "label": "E_A (V)"
    },
    "curves": [
     {
      "f": "300*(exp(x/3) - exp(-x/3))/(exp(x/3) + exp(-x/3))",
      "label": "n = 1200 rpm",
      "labelAt": 0.75
     }
    ],
    "height": 190
   }
  },
  {
   "title": "Equivalent circuit",
   "body": "The field circuit is $R_F$ in series with $L_F$; in steady state $V_F = R_FI_F$. The armature is $E_A$ in series with the armature resistance $R_A$. As a motor, $I_A$ flows into the + terminal of $E_A$, so $E_A < V_T$. In a **separately excited** machine the field has its own source.",
   "formula": "V_T = R_AI_A + E_A",
