   "id": "mc-dc-machines-1",
   "title": "Inside a DC machine",
   "kind": "learn",
   "steps": [
    {
     "id": "i1",
     "type": "info",
     "title": "Field, armature and commutator",
     "body": "The stator poles carry the **field winding** (or permanent magnets) and create a steady magnetic field. The rotor carries the **armature** conductors. Each armature coil must carry current in one direction under a north pole and the opposite direction under a south pole; the **commutator** and **brushes** switch it over as the rotor turns, so the torque keeps one direction."
    },
    {
     "id": "q1",
     "type": "matchPairs",
     "prompt": "Match each part to its job.",
     "pairs": [
      [
       "Commutator",
       "Reverses the current in each rotating coil"
      ],
      [
       "Brushes",
       "Sliding contact to the rotating armature"
      ],
      [
       "Field winding",
       "Sets up the magnetic flux"
      ],
      [
       "Armature winding",
       "Carries the load-dependent current"
      ]
     ],
     "explanation": "The field winding (stator) makes the flux. The armature (rotor) carries the current that produces torque. Brushes connect the external circuit to the commutator segments, which switch the coil currents as the rotor turns."
    },
    {
     "id": "i2",
     "type": "info",
     "title": "Back EMF and torque",
     "body": "A spinning armature in a field induces a voltage, the **back EMF** $E_A$. Current in the armature produces torque. Both are proportional to the flux, through the same machine constant $K\\phi$:",
     "formula": "E_A = K\\phi\\,\\omega_m,\\qquad T_{dev} = K\\phi\\,I_A"
    },
